The problem for UK businesses is data protection. DeepSeek''s privacy policy states that information is stored on servers in the People''s Republic of China, and several European regulators — including Italy''s data protection authority — have taken action against the app. Under UK GDPR, sending customer or employee personal data there raises international-transfer questions most SMEs cannot answer.

The safer route is the open-weight model: DeepSeek publishes model weights that can be run on UK or EU infrastructure you control, or accessed through established cloud providers that host it in-region. That removes the data-location issue, though you should still test outputs carefully.', 'https://www.deepseek.com', 'freemium', 'yes', 'yes', null, 6.5, 'DeepSeek''s models are impressively capable and free to use, but its consumer app stores data in China. We do not recommend it for confidential UK business data unless the open-weight model is self-hosted.', array['Non-sensitive experiments, or self-hosted open-weight use']::text[], array['Free and highly capable','Strong at maths and code','Open-weight models available','Very low API cost']::text[], array['Data stored in China (consumer app)','Regulatory action in several countries','Censorship on some topics','No business admin controls']::text[]) on conflict (slug) do update set company_id = excluded.company_id, name = excluded.name, status = excluded.status, short_description = excluded.short_description, long_description = excluded.long_description, website_url = excluded.website_url, pricing_model = excluded.pricing_model, free_plan = excluded.free_plan, api_available = excluded.api_available, open_source = excluded.open_source, editorial_score = excluded.editorial_score, editorial_notes = excluded.editorial_notes, best_for = excluded.best_for, pros = excluded.pros, cons = excluded.cons, updated_at = now();
